          %h3#monoidal_topological_categories Monoidal topological categories
          %p
            We recall the basic definitions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+a+monoidal+category"} monoids
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+object"} modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to monoidal categories. All examples are at the end of this section, starting with example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopAsASymmetricMonoidalCategory"}
            below.
          #MonoidalCategory.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                (pointed)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+category"} topologically enriched
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              is a (pointed)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+category"} topologically enriched category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              (
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#TopEnrichedCategory"}> def.
              ) equipped with
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a (pointed)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+functor"} topologically enriched functor
                  (
